    "lawCode": "Add §§77-a & 77-b, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S8055         Revised 06/14/16\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to the effect of\npower importation on electrical markets\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe bill is designed to examine the competitive disadvantages faced by\nNew York's state power generation facilities associated with the\nimportation of out of state power, and to ensure that the letter and\nspirit of renewable energy goals are enforced with regard to imported\npower.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 77-a subdivision 1 of the public service\nlaw to state legislative intent which specifies that it is in the best\neconomic and environmental interests of the state that to adopt\nconsistent policies that to the to the greatest extent possible to\nensure that environmental policy goals are enforced for any generator\nthat does business in the New York state energy markets.\n\nA new section 77-b is added to direct the public service commission,\nthrough a comprehensive monitoring and reporting methodology, to\ndetermine undue competitive advantages in the state's electricity\nmarkets that out-of-state power generators have over in-state power\ngenerators due to the state's imposition of charges, fees,\nassessments, environmental standards and/or allowance auctions of\nin-state generated power imported into the state. Such methodology\nshall be established pursuant to a stakeholder process through a\nproceeding established by the commission. The commission is further\ndirected to report the negative consequences of imported power as well\nas how these consequences could be minimized to the governor and the\nrespective chairs of the senate and assembly energy committees within\n9 months of establishing the methodology. The report must reflect\nstakeholder review and comment on the impact of imported power's\nimpact on local employment and revenues, and adverse environmental\nimpacts both within and outside the state.  Subdivision 2 provides\ndefinitions for \"competitive advantage\", \"in-state power generator\",\nand \"out-of-state power generator\".\n\nSection 2 provides that this act shall take effect 30 days after\nenactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nPower generators outside of New York that do business in New York,\nshould not obtain competitive advantages over in-state generators.\n\nIt is in the best interest of the state to adopt consistent policies\nthat, to the greatest extent possible, utilize electrical power in as\ncost efficient and environmentally friendly means possible whether the\npower is produced within or outside the state of New York.\n\nPolicies that encourage unfair competitive advantages for out of state\npower producers, threaten jobs and local tax bases and encourage the\nimportation of pollution intensive power into the state and are a\nsignificant threat to the health of New York residents.\n\nIn their efforts to reach environmental and renewable energy goals,\nNew York power regulators have increasingly opted to promote and\napprove transmission upgrades to the state's electrical grid that\nobviate the need for in-state generation of significant megawatts of\npower.\nInstead of promoting the repowering of power plants into cleaner\ntechnology or preservation of in-state generation, they have chosen in\nmany cases to endorse policies that export New York jobs, revenue, and\ncontrol of the power supply. These policies have favored transmission\nupgrades, and have had the consequence of encouraging major out of\nstate power producers to produce more energy, creating more harmful\nemissions and negatively impacting the environment.\n\nWhile these policy decisions appear to have lowered emissions to more\nquickly meet the state's emissions reduction goals, the irony is that\nin order to meet energy consumption demands of residents and\nbusinesses in the state, out of state power plants are increasing\ntheir energy production, and in many cases, are now producing more\n\"dirty\" power than would have been produced by instate generators that\nnow are going out of business.\n\nIn addition to the detrimental effects upon communities that would\noccur if most of our generation is replaced by transmission, PILOT\npayments will be at risk and this will create a great deal of angst\nand uncertainty in the community, limited power supply in the state\nand importation of dirty coal power from out of state will result.\n\nImporting power primarily through transmission upgrades as a priority\npolicy direction for the state is a detrimental direction to take. It\nwill hurt our communities and our in-state manufacturers.\nManufacturers need the ability to expand since expansion often entails\na need for more power. Manufacturers are dis-incentivized from\nexpanding their businesses in New York when there are no easy means to\nincreasing their power supply, and no base load power plants are\nnearby which is the result when power is imported via transmission\nlines rather than produced locally.\n\nA key factor that is seemingly not considered by power regulators who\nendorse transmission over instate generation, is that for every\nmegawatt of power that is imported there is an impact on emissions at\nother power plants. When dirtier power plants out of state have to\nproduce more energy, those power plants increase harmful emissions\nwhich hurts the environment more and is in opposition to the spirit\nand intent of New York's clean energy goals.\n\nThere are upwind and downwind emissions in the air that carry\npollution over New York's borders. Emissions actually are increased as\na result of power plants out of state increasing their power\nproduction to meet the need of New York's bellying-up fossil fuel\nplants.\n\nNew York has much higher standards for clean power production than\nmany other states. Replacing our in-state generation with transmission\nprojects only serves to import dirty power out of bordering states.\n\nThe public has a right to know what the sources of our power are and\nwhether emissions overall are truly being reduced. This bill will\nprovide a way for a full and accurate analysis of true sources of\npower and emissions, and will help state policymakers to make more\ninformed decisions that will better help to promote policies that are\nmore in line with the goals of promoting a clean environment while\npreserving New York energy independence.\n\nPolicymakers in the state should not advance policies that promote out\nof state generators to produce increased amounts of dirty power to\nmeet our state's power needs with costs borne on the backs of\nratepayers. Instead policies should be promoted that encourage private\ninvestment, job creation and clean technology repowering of power\nplants. Energy policies that preserve our communities' futures and\nserve our state's and the environment's best interests should be\nprioritized.\nThe analysis and reporting that this legislation requires will help to\nsteer energy policy in a direction that helps improve the environment\noverall, not just in New York, will save jobs, and encourage\nbusinesses to grow.\n\nReliability and congestion on the state's power grid cannot be as\nefficiently managed by simply importing power from out of state\ngeneration. In the case of emergency situations such as black-outs,\nattacks on the grid or spikes in demand, if we rely solely on\ntransmitted power, New York is in a weakened position to respond and\nsolve issues, because decisions are being made by authorities outside\nof our jurisdiction.\n\nIn contrast, a healthy balance between base load power generation,\nlocal renewable sources and reliable short and long range transmission\nin the state is needed to give New York the control that we need, to\nhelp electricity rates stay low, protect consumers and jobs and help\nassure a better environmental and economic future for our state.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nUndetermined\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
